{#-
  Merkl Distributor — claim accumulated campaign rewards.

  Grants exactly ONE function on the canonical per-chain Merkl Distributor:

    claim(address[] users, address[] tokens, uint256[] amounts, bytes32[][] proofs)

  Required template params:
    (none)

  Resolved from aliases (NOT params):
    aliases.merkl.distributor — the canonical Merkl Distributor for the
    active chain. One distributor per chain, fixed by the protocol, so
    there is nothing for a deployment to choose.

  ## Why every parameter is `pass`

  zac's param_type taxonomy (static | dynamic | tuple | abi_encoded) has no
  array type, so "every element of `users` equals the avatar" cannot be
  expressed here, even though Roles v2 itself supports array element
  conditions — the same class of DSL gap the lido template documents for
  ether allowances. The unscoped `users` below is that limitation made
  explicit, not an oversight.

  It is also not load-bearing, for two independent reasons in the deployed
  Distributor (verified against the mainnet implementation):

    1. Destination — through `claim` the recipients are forced to zero, so
       the distributor pays each users[i]'s rewards to users[i] itself, or
       to a claimRecipient users[i] has PREVIOUSLY set for that token,
       which only users[i] can do. The caller controls nothing about the
       destination. The Safe sets no claimRecipient, and this policy
       cannot set one (below), so the Safe's rewards can only land on the
       Safe.
    2. Authorisation — `_claim` reverts `NotWhitelisted()` when the caller
       is neither users[i] nor an operator users[i] approved (nor a Merkl
       main operator / governor). In practice the Safe can only claim for
       ITSELF; a claim naming a stranger reverts at the protocol unless
       that stranger explicitly approved the Safe — and even then the
       payout still routes per (1).

  Merkl `amounts` are CUMULATIVE totals by tree design: the distributor
  transfers `amount - alreadyClaimed`, so a replayed claim is a no-op
  rather than a double-pay.

  ## Deliberate omissions — the actual attack surface

    claimWithRecipient(users, tokens, amounts, proofs, recipients, datas)
      — the caller-chosen `recipients` IS the redirection vector: granted,
        it would pay the Safe's rewards to an arbitrary address.
    setClaimRecipient(recipient, token)
      — persistently reroutes every FUTURE claim of the Safe for `token`.
    toggleOperator(user, operator)
      — authorises an external operator to claim on the Safe's behalf,
        including through claimWithRecipient, outside this policy's gate.

  Everything else on the distributor (updateTree, dispute/revoke, the
  setters, recoverERC20, the upgrade path) is Merkl-governance-gated and
  also not granted. Security therefore rests on target + selector pinning
  plus these omissions, and on nothing else.
-#}
roles:
  - address: "{{ aliases.merkl.distributor }}"
    functions:
      - signature: "function claim(address[] users, address[] tokens, uint256[] amounts, bytes32[][] proofs)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: users
            param_type: "dynamic"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: tokens
            param_type: "dynamic"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: amounts
            param_type: "dynamic"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: proofs
            param_type: "dynamic"
            operator: "pass"
